2 Materials and Methods Mt clay was obtained from Sigma Aldrich (Missouri, USA). Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and polyethylene glycol sorbitan monooleate (Tween80) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich (Darmstadt, Germany). Ultrapure water was delivered from Elga Purelab Option Q (Anamed & Analitik Grup, Istanbul, Türkiye). Potato starch was purchased from Sigma Aldrich (Darmstadt, Germany), and glycerol was obtained from ISOLAB (Wertheim, Germany). Ethanol and acetic acid were supplied by Merck KGaA (Darmstadt, Germany). Aluminum standard solution (1000 µg/mL) was purchased from Chemlab (Zedelgem, Belgium). All chemicals used for the production of films are among authorized substances that are allowed for use in the manufacture of plastic food contact materials by the EU (Union  2025 ) and Turkish (Republic of Türkiye Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry  2024 ) regulations. 2.1 Organomodification Process of Mt oMt was prepared by a modification of the method used by Xie et al. ( 2012 ). Due to the hydrophilic structure of Mt clay, it was placed in drying oven (Universal UF110, Memmert GmbH + Co.KG, Schwabach, Germany) at 80°C for 12 h prior to use. Clay (3% w/w based on water) was weighed using analytical balance (Shimadzu ATX224, Shimadzu Corp., Kyoto, Japan) and dispersed in 50 mL of ultrapure water. Subsequently, surfactant was added, and the mixture was stirred with magnetic stirrer (RT15 power, IKA‐Werke GmbH & Co. KG, Staufen, Germany) at room temperature. Surfactant concentration percentage used for organomodification is given in Table  1 . TABLE 1 The type and amount of surfactants used for modification of montmorillonite clay. Surfactant type Modified nanoclay (organoclay) Surfactant percentage (% w/w nanoclay) — Mt (Blank) — CTAB 1‐CTAB‐oMt 4.2 2‐CTAB‐oMt 8.3 3‐CTAB‐oMt 16.7 4‐CTAB‐oMt 20.0 5‐CTAB‐oMt 23.3 6‐CTAB‐oMt 26.7 7‐CTAB‐oMt 30.0 8‐CTAB‐oMt 33.3 Tween80 1‐Tween‐oMt 4.2 2‐Tween‐oMt 8.3 3‐Tween‐oMt 16.7 4‐Tween‐oMt 33.3 
 Note : Mt: Unmodified (surfactant‐free) montmorillonite (blank) clay, CTAB‐oMt: Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide surfactant‐modified montmorillonite, Tween‐oMt: Tween80 surfactant‐modified montmorillonite. Increasing numbers in organoclay naming correspond to higher surfactant concentration in the structure. John Wiley & Sons, Ltd. Treated Mt suspension was centrifuged at 6000 rpm for 10 min using centrifuge (Universal 320R, Andreas Hettich GmbH & Co. KG, Tuttlingen, Germany). The settled part was repeatedly washed with ultrapure water and centrifuged to eliminate excess surfactant from the surface of modified clays. The material was then dried in oven at 80°C for 24 h, followed by grinding using mortar and grinder (Aromatic, Fakir Hausgeräte GmbH, Vaihingen/Enz, Germany). The preparation steps of organoclays are illustrated in Figure  1 . After grinding, oMt samples with cationic surfactant CTAB (CTAB‐oMt) and non‐ionic surfactant Tween80 (Tween‐oMt) were obtained and stored in desiccator for further use. As control (blank), nanoclay (Mt, unmodified/surfactant‐free clay) was dispersed in ultrapure water without adding any surfactant, and the same procedure was followed. FIGURE 1 The scheme of procedure to prepare organoclay. 2.2 Characterization of Organoclays In the present work, a Zetasizer (Zetasizer Nano ZSP, Malvern Instruments Ltd., Malvern, UK) was used to determine particle size analysis, zeta potential, and electrical conductivity. The organoclay solutions were examined by diluting with ultrapure water. A polystyrene‐folded capillary cell (DTS1070) was used in Zetasizer measurements, and it was ensured that no air bubbles adhered to cell walls (International Organization for Standardization  2017 ). Zetasizer experiments were conducted in triplicate at 25°C. The pH was measured using a digital pH meter (WTW Inolab pH 7110, Xylem Analytics Germany Sales GmbH & Co. KG, Weilheim, Germany). FT‐IR spectrometer equipped with an attenuated total reflectance (ATR) cell (Spectrum Two, Perkin Elmer Inc., Shelton, CT, USA) was used to obtain the spectra at a wavenumber of 4000–400 cm −1  by averaging 10 scans. XRD measurements were carried out using an XRD (D8 Discover, Bruker Corp., Billerica, MA, USA) with Cu K 
 α  radiation ( λ : 1.5406 Å). Scanning rate was set to 1°/min, having a scanning range (2 θ ) from 2° to 30° at room temperature. The interlayer distances of organoclays were calculated using Bragg's equation ( nλ  = 2d sin θ ). 2.3 Starch‐Based Nanocomposite Film Materials 2.3.1 Preparation of Film Materials To ensure film flexibility, glycerol was added as a plasticizer to the film‐forming solution to keep water molecules within polymer; the resulting matrix is known as thermoplastic starch (TPS) (Agarwal  2021 ). First, the amount of glycerol was determined on the basis of preliminary studies conducted without incorporation of nanofiller in structure. Starch was first dried in oven at 70°C for approximately 12 h to remove free water (Aouada et al.  2011 ). Glycerol was added to starch at varying dry weight ratios (30%, 40%, 50%, and 60% w/w, based on starch) and dissolved by mixing in water using a magnetic stirrer with a heating plate. Then, the mixture was poured into a Petri dish and dried at 30°C ± 2°C for 15 h. The quantity of plasticizer has a significant impact on macroscopic properties of starch‐based films (Agarwal  2021 ). The film material containing 40% glycerol ensured good processability and formation of uniform structure, with this concentration yielding the most favorable results in terms of overall appearance, exhibiting desirable macroscopic characteristics, such as smoothness, non‐brittleness, rigidity, sturdiness, flexibility, non‐sticky surface upon handling, and ease of detachment from the Petri dish. Starch nanocomposite films were subsequently produced by solvent casting method, as shown in Figure  2 . The preparation procedure for biocomposite materials was modified on the basis of techniques outlined by Aouada et al. ( 2011 ) and Suryanto et al. ( 2019 ). Dried starch (5.0% w/v, based on final volume of solution) and glycerol (40% w/w, based on starch) were weighed and mixed with 50 mL of ultrapure water. The mixture was stirred using a magnetic stirrer for 1 h at room temperature (Mixture A). Clay concentration in the matrix was studied at levels of 3% and 5% (w/w, based on starch). A known quantity of organoclay and surfactant‐free clay was weighed, and 50 mL of ultrapure water was added (Mixture B). Mixture B was stirred for 16 h at room temperature on a magnetic stirrer, then ultrasonic treatment (35 kHz, 860 W, 60 min) was performed in an ultrasonic bath (Sonorex RK514BH, BANDELIN Electronic GmbH & Co. KG, Berlin, Germany) to prevent agglomeration of clay in the biocomposite material and to ensure homogeneous distribution. Mixtures A and B were then combined and stirred for 1 h at room temperature. As control film (S), starch and glycerol were weighed, and 100 mL of ultrapure water was put in without adding clay/organoclay, followed by stirring for 2 h. After stirring for 20 min at gelatinization temperature (65–70°C), 25 mL of film‐forming solution was cast onto Petri dishes (90 × 17 mm 2 ) and allowed to dry at 30°C ± 2°C for 24 h in oven. The dried films were removed from Petri dishes and kept in a desiccator until measurements were conducted. The thickness of film materials was 0.187 ± 0.017 mm. FIGURE 2 The scheme of the procedure to prepare starch‐based nanocomposite film materials. 2.3.2 Water Absorption Capacity and Water Solubility Water absorption capacity (water uptake) and water solubility (soluble matter lost during immersion) of films were determined according to the American Society for Testing and Materials ( 2022 ), with the modification for sample size as proposed by Abdullah and Dong ( 2019 ). Text specimens, with a size of 2 × 2 cm 2 , were conditioned in an oven for 24 h at 50°C ± 2°C. Samples were then cooled in a desiccator and weighed to obtain conditioned weight ( W C 
 ). Conditioned test samples were fully immersed in labeled containers of ultrapure water at 23°C ± 1°C for 2 h in a climate chamber (HPP750Eco Model, Memmert GmbH + Co. KG, Schwabach, Germany). At the end of test period, test samples were removed from water; surface water was gently dried with filter paper and weighed to obtain wet weight ( W W 
 ). Three samples for each material were tested. The percentage of increase in weight during immersion (water absorption,  W A 
 ) was calculated according to

 (1) 
 W A % = W W − W C W C 

 Starch‐based films have water‐soluble components due to free hydroxyl groups in their structure (Agarwal  2021 ). The materials that are suspected to contain water‐soluble components should be reconditioned for same test conditions as the initial drying process after immersion in ultrapure water. Swollen samples from water absorption capacity test were dried at 50°C ± 2°C for 24 h in an oven for reconditioning. Samples were then cooled in a desiccator and reweighed to obtain reconditioned weight ( W R 
 ). The percentage water‐soluble matter lost during immersion (water solubility,  W S 
 ) was calculated according to

 (2) 
 W S % = W C − W R W C 

 2.3.3 Migration Analyses For migration analyses, test conditions (40°C—30 min) were chosen according to current regulations in the EU and Türkiye, representing short periods (≤30 min) of food contact at cold or room temperatures (Union  2025 ; Republic of Türkiye Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry  2024 ). The migration analyses were conducted on the basis of the total immersion method. 2.3.3.1 Overall Migration Analysis Addition of organoclay into starch polymer was evaluated for aqueous and acidic food simulants. Overall (total) migration analyses were carried out according to the standards (European Committee for Standardization  2006 ;  2022 ). For acidic food simulant, a 3% acetic acid (w/v) solution was prepared by diluting 30 g of acetic acid with ultrapure water to obtain a final volume of 1 L. For aqueous (hydrophilic) simulant, a 10% ethanol (v/v) solution was prepared according to Gay‐Lussac's table for alcohol dilution. First, clean glass Petri dishes were labeled for identification and placed in an oven at 105–110°C to dry for 60 min. Then, dishes were removed from an oven and kept in a desiccator to allow cooling to room temperature. The mass of each dish was recorded using an analytical balance. Glass containers for each blank and test sample were labeled for identification. The study was carried out in three replicates of test samples, along with two blank containers containing food simulant without film material under the same conditions. Simulant of 100 mL was placed into each container (without test specimen), closed with stoppers, and placed in the oven at the specified test temperature (40°C ± 1°C) and allowed to remain until the test temperature was reached, then 1 dm 2  of film material was immersed in each glass container filled with simulant. This step of the procedure was carried out in the minimum time possible to avoid unnecessary heat loss. The containers were again closed with stoppers and placed back in the oven at 40°C. Oven temperature was monitored to reach test temperature as quickly as possible, approximately within 5 min. Once the food simulant reached the test temperature, the containers were left for 30 min. At the end of testing time, they were removed from the oven, and test specimens were carefully removed from the simulant. The exposed food simulant was poured into a marked glass Petri dish. Evaporation of the food simulant was performed over a hot plate in a fume cupboard. Each container that contained food simulant was rinsed with two portions of 10 mL of fresh simulant, and the evaporation process was continued. When the simulant had evaporated, glass dishes were placed in the oven at 105–110°C for 60 min to complete the evaporation process and dry any remaining residue. Then, dishes were taken out of the oven and kept in a desiccator to allow cooling to room temperature. The mass of each dish was recorded using an analytical balance until the difference between two consecutive measurements was no more than 0.5 mg. Total migration results were calculated gravimetrically as mass of residue (mg) per food contact surface area of sample (dm 2 ) (European Committee for Standardization  2022 ). 2.3.3.2 Specific Migration Analysis Aluminum was selected as a marker to check the specific migration profile of clay/organoclay in starch‐based nanocomposites, as Al 3+  is a common hydrated cation located between Mt layers (Mauricio‐Iglesias et al.  2010 ). Specific migration from the obtained nanocomposite films into simulants was determined through a test procedure conducted in two stages (European Committee for Standardization  2004 ). The first stage was exposure of test material to acidic and hydrophilic simulants as outlined in the overall migration analysis. The total immersion method was essentially the same, except the surface area of material was determined according to the European Committee for Standardization ( 2004 ), with films immersed in 100 mL of food simulant, corresponding to 0.6 dm 2 . Materials were exposed to food simulant at specified times and temperatures under experimental conditions. Analysis was carried out in three replicates of test materials and two blanks. The second stage involved the determination of migrant (Al) in simulants. At the end of exposure time, samples were carefully removed, and simulant in each container was analyzed for aluminum migration using ICP‐MS (Agilent 7700x, Agilent Technologies Inc., California, USA). The working range for the aluminum element in ICP‐MS was established as 0–100 µg/L. When the results exceeded the calibration curve range, a 1:5 dilution was performed, and samples were reanalyzed using the instrument. In cases where dilution was applied, the specific migration result was calculated by multiplying the measured value by the dilution factor. The lower limit of quantification (LOQ) in the method was 0.0083 ppm for acidic simulant and 0.0134 ppm for hydrophilic simulant. The concentration range of linearity was checked ( R 
 2  > 0.999). Specific migration results were calculated as mg migrant (Al) released per kg of food simulant (mg/kg) (European Committee for Standardization  2004 ; Nordic Committee on Food Analysis  2007 ; Republic of Türkiye Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry  2024 ). 2.4 Statistical Analysis The results were statistically evaluated by using SPSS Statistics 29 program (IBM SPSS Statistics). For statistical analysis of data, analysis of variance (ANOVA) and Duncan's multiple range test were used.

